  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nUpper ridging was still over the southern plains per the 07Z water \nvapor imagery, keeping the mean westerlies and storm tracks to the \nnorth of the forecast area. Surface obs placed a trough of low \npressure from the northern plains into the central high plains. A \ndecent pressure gradient over central and eastern KS has kept winds \nand temperatures up so far this morning. \n\nNot a lot of change to the forecast as ensembles and the NBM \ncontinue to show the upper ridging persisting over the southern and \ncentral plains through the forecast period. There is reasonable \nagreement from the operational GFS/ECMWF in the overall synoptic \npattern but the ECMWF does tend to keep a stronger ridge in terms of \n500MB heights over the plains. The interesting thing to watch for is \nthe 00Z AIGFS develops a deeper upper trough over the northeast \nwhich tends to shift the upper ridge to the west of the forecast \narea and develops a more meridional pattern along the MS valley. \nPresumably this would allow for some cooler air from the north to \nfilter into the area. But given limited experience with the AIGFS, \nam not comfortable straying from the NBM. So overall a dry and hot \nforecast continues. The latest NBM keeps POPs less than 20 percent \nthrough the period. There is a signal from the RAP for some \nisentropic lift near the NEB state line this morning with pretty \nsteep mid level lapse rates. CAMs are not to aggressive with \ndeveloping elevated showers so have kept POPs around 10 percent and \nwill monitor trends as the morning progresses. \n\nThe main question is whether or not to go with a heat advisory. \nThere is good agreement in the operational solutions for hot \ntemperatures and based on 925MB and 850MB temps progs have today's \nforecast highs around 100 degrees. But good south southwesterly \nunidirectional flow is forecast to mix out boundary layer moisture \nthis afternoon. Because of this, heat indices are expected to be \npretty close to the actual temperature. With most areas seeing \nindices in the 100 to 103 range and humidity being lower than what \nwe experienced earlier in the summer, will hold off on any advisory \nfor now. Operational models suggest with the 1000-500MB thickness \nprogs that highs may be a degree or so less hot to begin the \nworkweek. So in general it looks like persistent highs in the upper \n90s and around 100 with lower humidity should keep really oppressive \nconditions on the lower end of possibilities. Later shifts can \nreassess the need for heat headlines.",
